const body = `<h1>8.1b — 2D Arrays: Read, Write, and Navigate Coordinates</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Grid Editor</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you saw that 2D arrays use [row][col] indexing. Today you'll write code that reads, writes, and validates grid positions.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>GridEditor</code> with <code>int[][] grid = new int[5][5]</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Fill the grid so that <code>grid[row][col] = row * col</code>.</li>
            <li>Print the grid with row and column headers.</li>
            <li>Method <code>int getValue(int row, int col)</code>: return the value. If the coordinates are out of bounds, return -1.</li>
            <li>Method <code>void setValue(int row, int col, int value)</code>: set the value if in bounds, print an error otherwise.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Boundary Check</h3>
        <p>Try <code>getValue(5, 0)</code> and <code>getValue(0, 5)</code>. Which index is out of bounds? How do you determine the number of rows and columns dynamically (hint: <code>grid.length</code> and <code>grid[0].length</code>)?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-basics.html">2D Arrays</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
